      "model": "claude-opus-5-5",
      "question": "The last maintenance job on Transformer 1 at site MAIN was supposed to deal with the gassing, and I want to know whether it actually worked.\n\nFind the most recently closed work order on Transformer 1. Give me its number, description, completion date, resolution notes and failure code, and tell me which fault it was meant to fix.\n\nThen look at the oil samples taken after that date, within 2024-01-15T00:00:00.000000 to 2024-01-19T00:00:00.000000. Use hydrogen, methane, ethane, ethylene, acetylene and co. Screen hydrogen, acetylene and co for anomalous samples with a proper anomaly detection model rather than a fixed threshold. Separately, run a dissolved gas fault classification on the latest complete sample and compare its fault class with the fault the job was meant to fix.\n\nDecide as follows:\n- If both checks point to trouble, meaning at least one anomaly is flagged and the classification still shows the same fault or a higher-energy one, call the fix ineffective. Create a priority 2 corrective follow-up work order that references the original work order number, and code it with the closest failure code from our catalog.\n- If only one of the two checks points to trouble, don't create a corrective job. Recommend a confirmatory oil resample within 7 days and say which check disagreed.\n- If neither points to trouble, say the fix appears to have held.\n\nIf the closed job was oil filtration, degassing or regeneration, the gas levels will have been reset. In that case, judge the result on the rate of change after the job, not on absolute levels, and say you did so.\n\nIf no work order on Transformer 1 was closed before or during the window, say so, use all samples in the window, and give only the classification and anomaly result without a verdict on any fix. If fewer than 4 samples fall after the completion date, skip the anomaly model and compare the first and last post-job values instead.",
      "answer": "I couldn't answer whether the fix worked: no work order on Transformer 1 has been closed, so there's no job to judge. The gas pattern is a thermal fault (T2, 300\u2013700 \u00b0C). I couldn't run the anomaly model, so that check is still missing.\n\n**1. Most recent closed work order: none.** Transformer 1 has three work orders, and none has been closed. There is no completion date, resolution notes, failure code or intended fault to report.\n\n| WO | Description | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1000051 | OLTC (tap changer) inspection, suspected contact wear/coking | APPR (approved, not done) |\n| 1000052 | Confirmatory DGA oil sample | APPR (approved, not done) |\n| 1000053 | Outage and inspection, 2024-01-20..21 | WAPPR (awaiting approval) |\n\nAs you asked for this case, I used all samples in the window, gave no verdict on a fix, and created no work order. The oil filtration/degassing rule doesn't apply either.\n\n**2. Samples in the window (4 daily samples, 2024-01-15 to 2024-01-18)**\n\n| Date | H2 | CH4 | C2H6 | C2H4 | C2H2 | CO |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 01-15 | 2845 | 7406 | 5467 | 16684 | 7 | 32 |\n| 01-16 | 2950 | 7200 | 5400 | 16500 | 6 | 30 |\n| 01-17 | 2800 | 7300 | 5500 | 16700 | 8 | 35 |\n| 01-18 | 2900 | 7350 | 5450 | 16600 | 7 | 33 |\n\n**3. Fault classification on the latest sample (2024-01-18): thermal fault T2, 300\u2013700 \u00b0C.**\n- The DGA classification tool failed twice (\"LLM unavailable\"), so I worked out the IEC 60599 ratios by hand:\n  - CH4/H2 = 2.53\n  - C2H2/C2H4 = 0.0004\n  - C2H4/C2H6 = 3.05\n- That pattern is overheating, not arcing. It matches the \"DGA T2 thermal\" note on WO 1000051.\n- Levels are very high but steady across the four days. Total combustible gas is about 32,340 ppm, IEEE C57.104 Condition 4.\n\n**4. Anomaly screen (hydrogen, acetylene, CO): not run.** The model catalog has no anomaly-detection models, and the tool needs the samples saved as a data file. I have no way to create that file here. I didn't swap in a fixed threshold. Earlier anomaly runs on this transformer are logged, but they don't show what data was used, so I didn't rely on them.\n\nTo finish the screen, save the four samples above as a CSV (timestamp, hydrogen, acetylene, co). I can then run a local-outlier-factor detector on it (sktime SubLOF).",
      "score": {
